abstract: >-
  uSTAT is a free, browser-based statistical analysis platform aimed at
  clinicians, biostatisticians, and medical researchers. It spans hypothesis
  testing, regression (linear, logistic, Firth, ordinal, Poisson, negative
  binomial, GEE, mixed models, restricted cubic splines), and survival
  analysis including Cox with time-varying covariates, Kaplan-Meier,
  Fine-Gray competing risks, RMST, landmark analysis, shared frailty,
  recurrent events, and multi-state models with dynamic prediction.
  Causal-inference tools cover propensity score matching with Austin 2011
  diagnostics, IPTW, instrumental variables, mediation,
  difference-in-differences, regression discontinuity, and sensitivity for
  unmeasured confounding. It also provides diagnostic accuracy (ROC with
  DeLong comparison, calibration, decision-curve analysis, NRI/IDI),
  method agreement, meta-analysis, missing-data handling with MICE and MNAR
  sensitivity, factor analysis, time series, Bayesian tests, Table 1, and
  power calculations. Tree-ensemble machine learning is present but marked
  under development: it has not been checked against a reference
  implementation and its output should be treated as exploratory. A defined
  set of core tests
  (t-test, Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis, one-way ANOVA, correlation,
  chi-square, Fisher, logistic odds ratios, Cox hazard ratios and log-rank)
  is checked for numerical agreement with the SciPy, statsmodels and
  lifelines routines used to compute them, and reported test statistics are
  additionally verified to be internally consistent, so that the quoted p
  can be recovered from the quoted statistic and degrees of freedom. This
  covers correct use of those libraries rather than constituting independent
  validation of the methods themselves. Every analysis emits
  publication-ready methods text plus the equivalent R code.
  Statistical computation runs server-side, so an uploaded dataset is held
  in the backend's process memory; it is not written to disk in the default
  configuration and is cleared 30 minutes after the user's last activity by
  a reaper thread that runs irrespective of traffic. Both the retention
  period and the opt-in disk cache are set by environment variable.
